  "nameStory": "The Dark-winged Sweat Bee gets its name from two key features: its noticeably darker wings compared to many other sweat bees, and its membership in the sweat bee family. Like all sweat bees, it's called a 'sweat bee' because these tiny pollinators are often attracted to the salt in human perspiration on hot summer days. The scientific name 'fuscipenne' literally means 'dark-winged' in Latin, perfectly capturing this bee's distinguishing feature.",
  "description": "The Dark-winged Sweat Bee is a small, solitary native bee that plays an important role pollinating wildflowers throughout New England. These compact bees are typically dark-colored with a metallic sheen, measuring about the size of a small housefly. Unlike honeybees, they don't live in large colonies but instead create individual ground nests. You'll often spot them busily visiting flowers on sunny days, their dark wings catching the light as they move from bloom to bloom.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark Wings",
      "description": "The wings appear noticeably darker or more smoky than those of most other sweat bees, especially when viewed against bright sunlight. Hold your hand behind the bee to better see this dusky wing coloration.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Metallic Body Sheen",
      "description": "The body has a subtle metallic luster that shifts between dark bronze and greenish tones as the bee moves. This shimmer is most visible when sunlight hits the bee at different angles.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Compact Size",
      "description": "About the size of a large ant or small housefly, much smaller than a honeybee. When it lands on your skin, it feels surprisingly light and delicate.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground-hovering Behavior",
      "description": "Often seen hovering low over bare soil or sparse vegetation, especially near their nesting sites. They have a characteristic quick, darting flight pattern close to the ground.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flower Visiting Pattern",
      "description": "Moves quickly between small flowers, spending only seconds on each bloom. You'll hear a soft, high-pitched buzzing that's much quieter than a honeybee's hum.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Dark-winged Sweat Bees in open areas with well-drained, sandy or loamy soil where they can excavate their ground nests. They frequent sunny meadows, old fields, garden edges, and disturbed areas with exposed earth. You'll often find them visiting wildflowers like asters, goldenrod, and members of the sunflower family in these habitats.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults begin emerging in late spring as temperatures warm. Early individuals focus on mating and establishing nests in suitable soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when adults are most visible visiting flowers for nectar and pollen. This is the best time to observe their characteristic dark wings and foraging behavior.",
    "fall": "Activity continues into early fall, with late-season flowers providing important resources. Some individuals may still be active on warm autumn days.",
    "winter": "Adults die off and the species overwinters as larvae or pupae in underground nest cells, completely hidden from view."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called 'sweat bees,' these tiny pollinators are actually more interested in flowers than your perspiration—they only occasionally visit humans for the minerals in sweat when flowers are scarce, and they're so gentle they rarely sting even when handled.",
  "confusionNotes": "With dozens of Lasioglossum species in New England, positive identification often requires expert examination. However, the consistently dark wings help distinguish this species from relatives like the White-banded Sweat Bee (which has clear wings with white markings) or the Deep-blue Sweat Bee (which has a more obviously metallic blue-green body).",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The genus Lasioglossum is incredibly diverse in New England, with over 40 species recorded in the region. Many of these tiny bees look remarkably similar, which is why the 'dark wings' feature is so helpful for this particular species—it's one of the few field-identifiable characteristics that doesn't require a microscope.",
  "compareHints": "Among its many Lasioglossum relatives, the Dark-winged Sweat Bee stands out primarily for its wing coloration—most other species in this genus have clear or lightly tinted wings. While species like the Deep-blue Sweat Bee may have more dramatic metallic body colors, and others like the White-banded Sweat Bee have distinctive white markings, L. fuscipenne's consistently dusky wings make it one of the more recognizable members of this challenging genus.",
